{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-17551" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The substitution $z={y}^{-1}$ can be used to transform the equation \n\n$$\nx\\frac{dy}{dx}={y}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-y\n$$\n\ninto an equation of the form ${z}^{′}(x)+P(x)z(x)=Q(x).$ What is the function $P(x)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043377/state.json" "file_sha256" "de6f5601e572e1df01884569e7966a5eb04e4de25587a1d15b5e8d41ae8120a0"} {"question" "q-17551" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ with the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=-{y}^{-2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-{z}^{-2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, the original differential equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(-{z}^{-2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}) & ={z}^{-2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-{z}^{-1} \\\\ -x{z}^{-2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & ={z}^{-2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-{z}^{-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now write the above equation in standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x).\n$$\n\nIn doing so, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x}+\\frac{1}{x}z \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{1}{x}z & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x)=-\\frac{1}{x}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043377/state.json" "file_sha256" "de6f5601e572e1df01884569e7966a5eb04e4de25587a1d15b5e8d41ae8120a0"} {"question" "q-17551" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043377/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "735b4c6e66964dc742ea9ae660ff8a26654509de6cde745dd5f9c25d42d1ddf3"} {"question" "q-17551" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043377/state.json" "file_sha256" "de6f5601e572e1df01884569e7966a5eb04e4de25587a1d15b5e8d41ae8120a0"} {"question" "q-17551" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{1}{x}"] ["math" "-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x}"] ["math" "\\operatorname{ln}⁡x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043377/state.json" "file_sha256" "de6f5601e572e1df01884569e7966a5eb04e4de25587a1d15b5e8d41ae8120a0"} {"question" "q-17551" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{1}{x}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043377/state.json" "file_sha256" "de6f5601e572e1df01884569e7966a5eb04e4de25587a1d15b5e8d41ae8120a0"} {"question" "q-167191" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The substitution $z={y}^{3}$ can be used to transform the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{y}^{2}}-{x}^{2}y\n$$\n\ninto an equation of the form ${z}^{′}(x)+P(x)z(x)=Q(x).$ What is the function $P(x)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043377/state.json" "file_sha256" "de6f5601e572e1df01884569e7966a5eb04e4de25587a1d15b5e8d41ae8120a0"} {"question" "q-167191" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{3}\\,⟹\\,y={z}^{1/3}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=3{y}^{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{3{z}^{2/3}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nConsequently, the original differential equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\frac{1}{3{z}^{2/3}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}) & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{z}^{2/3}}-{x}^{2}{z}^{1/3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-3{x}^{2}z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we write the above equation in standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x).\n$$\n\nDoing so, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-3{x}^{2}z \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+3{x}^{2}z & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x)=3{x}^{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043377/state.json" "file_sha256" "de6f5601e572e1df01884569e7966a5eb04e4de25587a1d15b5e8d41ae8120a0"} {"question" "q-167191" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043377/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "735b4c6e66964dc742ea9ae660ff8a26654509de6cde745dd5f9c25d42d1ddf3"} {"question" "q-167191" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043377/state.json" "file_sha256" "de6f5601e572e1df01884569e7966a5eb04e4de25587a1d15b5e8d41ae8120a0"} {"question" "q-167191" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x"] ["math" "3{x}^{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}"] ["math" "{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x"] ["math" "{x}^{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043377/state.json" "file_sha256" "de6f5601e572e1df01884569e7966a5eb04e4de25587a1d15b5e8d41ae8120a0"} {"question" "q-167191" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3{x}^{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043377/state.json" "file_sha256" "de6f5601e572e1df01884569e7966a5eb04e4de25587a1d15b5e8d41ae8120a0"} {"question" "q-17553" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The substitution $z={y}^{-1}$ can be used to transform the equation \n\n$$\n-x\\frac{dy}{dx}={y}^{2}-y\n$$\n\ninto an equation of the form ${z}^{′}(x)+P(x)z(x)=Q(x).$ What is the function $P(x)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043377/state.json" "file_sha256" "de6f5601e572e1df01884569e7966a5eb04e4de25587a1d15b5e8d41ae8120a0"} {"question" "q-17553" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ with the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=-{y}^{-2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-{z}^{-2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, the original differential equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-x(-{z}^{-2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}) & ={z}^{-2}-{z}^{-1} \\\\ x{z}^{-2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & ={z}^{-2}-{z}^{-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now write the above equation in standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x).\n$$\n\nIn doing so, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{x}-\\frac{1}{x}z \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{x}z & =\\frac{1}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x)=\\frac{1}{x}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043377/state.json" "file_sha256" "de6f5601e572e1df01884569e7966a5eb04e4de25587a1d15b5e8d41ae8120a0"} {"question" "q-17553" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043377/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "735b4c6e66964dc742ea9ae660ff8a26654509de6cde745dd5f9c25d42d1ddf3"} {"question" "q-17553" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043377/state.json" "file_sha256" "de6f5601e572e1df01884569e7966a5eb04e4de25587a1d15b5e8d41ae8120a0"} {"question" "q-17553" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}"] ["math" "2x"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{x}"] ["math" "x"] ["math" "{x}^{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043377/state.json" "file_sha256" "de6f5601e572e1df01884569e7966a5eb04e4de25587a1d15b5e8d41ae8120a0"} {"question" "q-17553" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{x}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043377/state.json" "file_sha256" "de6f5601e572e1df01884569e7966a5eb04e4de25587a1d15b5e8d41ae8120a0"} {"question" "q-79768" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The substitution $z=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y$ can be used to transform the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+y\\operatorname{ln}⁡y=xy\n$$\n\ninto an equation of the form ${z}^{′}(x)+P(x)z(x)=Q(x).$ What is the function $P(x)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043377/state.json" "file_sha256" "de6f5601e572e1df01884569e7966a5eb04e4de25587a1d15b5e8d41ae8120a0"} {"question" "q-79768" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{z}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nConsequently, the original differential equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{z}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+z{e}^{z}=x{e}^{z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now write the above equation in standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x).\n$$\n\nIn doing so, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{z}(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+z) & =x{e}^{z} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+z & =x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x)=1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043377/state.json" "file_sha256" "de6f5601e572e1df01884569e7966a5eb04e4de25587a1d15b5e8d41ae8120a0"} {"question" "q-79768" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043377/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "735b4c6e66964dc742ea9ae660ff8a26654509de6cde745dd5f9c25d42d1ddf3"} {"question" "q-79768" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043377/state.json" "file_sha256" "de6f5601e572e1df01884569e7966a5eb04e4de25587a1d15b5e8d41ae8120a0"} {"question" "q-79768" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{1}{x}"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "x"] ["math" "x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043377/state.json" "file_sha256" "de6f5601e572e1df01884569e7966a5eb04e4de25587a1d15b5e8d41ae8120a0"} {"question" "q-79768" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043377/state.json" "file_sha256" "de6f5601e572e1df01884569e7966a5eb04e4de25587a1d15b5e8d41ae8120a0"} {"question" "q-166602" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The substitution $z={y}^{-3}$ can be used to transform the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={y}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\n$$\n\ninto an equation of the form ${z}^{′}(x)+P(x)z(x)=Q(x).$ What is the function $P(x)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043377/state.json" "file_sha256" "de6f5601e572e1df01884569e7966a5eb04e4de25587a1d15b5e8d41ae8120a0"} {"question" "q-166602" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{-3}\\,⟹\\,y={z}^{-1/3}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=-3{y}^{-4}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{3{z}^{4/3}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nConsequently, the original differential equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3{z}^{4/3}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{{z}^{4/3}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\frac{1}{{z}^{1/3}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now write the above equation in standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x).\n$$\n\nIn doing so, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+3z\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-3z\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043377/state.json" "file_sha256" "de6f5601e572e1df01884569e7966a5eb04e4de25587a1d15b5e8d41ae8120a0"} {"question" "q-166602" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043377/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "735b4c6e66964dc742ea9ae660ff8a26654509de6cde745dd5f9c25d42d1ddf3"} {"question" "q-166602" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043377/state.json" "file_sha256" "de6f5601e572e1df01884569e7966a5eb04e4de25587a1d15b5e8d41ae8120a0"} {"question" "q-166602" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x"] ["math" "-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x"] ["math" "3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x"] ["math" "\\operatorname{tan}⁡x"] ["math" "{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043377/state.json" "file_sha256" "de6f5601e572e1df01884569e7966a5eb04e4de25587a1d15b5e8d41ae8120a0"} {"question" "q-166602" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043377/state.json" "file_sha256" "de6f5601e572e1df01884569e7966a5eb04e4de25587a1d15b5e8d41ae8120a0"} {"question" "q-79789" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the general solution to the following equation. \n\n$$\nx\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y=\\frac{4{x}^{2}}{y}\\,,\\,y>0\n$$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the substitution $z={y}^{2}-{x}^{2}.$*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043377/state.json" "file_sha256" "de6f5601e572e1df01884569e7966a5eb04e4de25587a1d15b5e8d41ae8120a0"} {"question" "q-79789" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{2}-{x}^{2}\\,⟹\\,y={(z+{x}^{2})}^{1/2}\\,.\n$$\n\n]\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=2y\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-2x\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2(z+{x}^{2})^{1/2}}(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+2x).\n$$\n\n \n\nConsequently, the original differential equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{2(z+{x}^{2})^{1/2}}(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+2x)+(z+{x}^{2})^{1/2} & =\\frac{4{x}^{2}}{(z+{x}^{2})^{1/2}} \\\\ x(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+2x)+2(z+{x}^{2}) & =8{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is linear, so we can use the method of integrating factors. First, we write the above equation in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+2x+\\frac{2}{x}(z+{x}^{2}) & =8x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{2}{x}z & =4x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x)=\\frac{2}{x},\\,Q(x)=4x\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int 2/x\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the standard form equation by $I(x)={x}^{2}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{2}{x}\\,z) & ={x}^{2}\\cdot 4x \\\\ {x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+2xz & =4{x}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}z) & =4{x}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}z)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 4{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {x}^{2}z & ={x}^{4}+C \\\\ z & ={x}^{2}+\\frac{C}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine $y(x)$ by writing $z={y}^{2}-{x}^{2}.$ This results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-{x}^{2} & ={x}^{2}+\\frac{C}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {y}^{2} & =2{x}^{2}+\\frac{C}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ y & =\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+\\frac{C}{{x}^{2}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we used that $y>0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043377/state.json" "file_sha256" "de6f5601e572e1df01884569e7966a5eb04e4de25587a1d15b5e8d41ae8120a0"} {"question" "q-79789" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043377/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "735b4c6e66964dc742ea9ae660ff8a26654509de6cde745dd5f9c25d42d1ddf3"} {"question" "q-79789" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043377/state.json" "file_sha256" "de6f5601e572e1df01884569e7966a5eb04e4de25587a1d15b5e8d41ae8120a0"} {"question" "q-79789" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=2x+\\frac{C}{{x}^{2}}"] ["math" "y=\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+\\frac{C}{{x}^{2}}}"] ["math" "y=\\sqrt{C{x}^{2}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}}"] ["math" "y=\\sqrt{x+\\frac{C}{x}}"] ["math" "y={x}^{2}+\\frac{C}{x}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043377/state.json" "file_sha256" "de6f5601e572e1df01884569e7966a5eb04e4de25587a1d15b5e8d41ae8120a0"} {"question" "q-79789" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+\\frac{C}{{x}^{2}}}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043377/state.json" "file_sha256" "de6f5601e572e1df01884569e7966a5eb04e4de25587a1d15b5e8d41ae8120a0"} {"question" "q-79784" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the general solution to the following equation. \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=8x{y}^{1/2}-\\frac{4}{x}y\\,,\\,y>0.\n$$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the substitution $z={y}^{1/2}.$*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043377/state.json" "file_sha256" "de6f5601e572e1df01884569e7966a5eb04e4de25587a1d15b5e8d41ae8120a0"} {"question" "q-79784" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{1/2}\\,⟹\\,y={z}^{2}\\,.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{dz}{dx}=\\frac{1}{2{y}^{1/2}}\\frac{dy}{dx}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{dy}{dx}=2z\\frac{dz}{dx}.\n$$\n\n \n\nConsequently, the original differential equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2z\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =8xz-\\frac{4}{x}{z}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =4x-\\frac{2}{x}z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is linear, so we can use the method of integrating factors. First, we write the above equation in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =4x-\\frac{2}{x}z \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{2}{x}z & =4x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x)=\\frac{2}{x},\\,Q(x)=4x\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (2/x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the standard form equation by $I(x)={x}^{2}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{2}{x}z) & ={x}^{2}\\cdot (4x) \\\\ {x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+2xz & =4{x}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}z) & =4{x}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}z)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 4{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {x}^{2}z & ={x}^{4}+C \\\\ z & =\\frac{{x}^{4}+C}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine $y(x)$ by writing $z={y}^{1/2}.$ This results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{1/2} & =\\frac{{x}^{4}+C}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ y & =\\frac{({x}^{4}+C)^{2}}{{x}^{4}}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043377/state.json" "file_sha256" "de6f5601e572e1df01884569e7966a5eb04e4de25587a1d15b5e8d41ae8120a0"} {"question" "q-79784" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043377/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "735b4c6e66964dc742ea9ae660ff8a26654509de6cde745dd5f9c25d42d1ddf3"} {"question" "q-79784" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043377/state.json" "file_sha256" "de6f5601e572e1df01884569e7966a5eb04e4de25587a1d15b5e8d41ae8120a0"} {"question" "q-79784" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=\\frac{({x}^{4}+C)^{2}}{{x}^{4}}"] ["math" "y=\\frac{{x}^{2}+C}{{x}^{2}}"] ["math" "y=\\frac{{x}^{4}+C}{{x}^{2}}"] ["math" "y=\\frac{{x}^{8}+C}{{x}^{2}}"] ["math" "y=\\frac{{x}^{8}+C}{{x}^{4}}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043377/state.json" "file_sha256" "de6f5601e572e1df01884569e7966a5eb04e4de25587a1d15b5e8d41ae8120a0"} {"question" "q-79784" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=\\frac{({x}^{4}+C)^{2}}{{x}^{4}}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043377/state.json" "file_sha256" "de6f5601e572e1df01884569e7966a5eb04e4de25587a1d15b5e8d41ae8120a0"} {"question" "q-17546" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the general solution to the following equation. \n\n$$\n2(1+{x}^{2})\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{y}-2xy\\,,\\,y>0\n$$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the substitution $z={y}^{2}.$*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043377/state.json" "file_sha256" "de6f5601e572e1df01884569e7966a5eb04e4de25587a1d15b5e8d41ae8120a0"} {"question" "q-17546" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{2}\\,⟹\\,y={z}^{1/2}\\,.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $y(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2{z}^{1/2}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, the original differential equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(1+{x}^{2})}{{z}^{1/2}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{{z}^{1/2}}-2x{z}^{1/2} \\\\ (1+{x}^{2})\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =1-2xz\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is linear, so we can use the method of integrating factors. First, we write the above equation in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1+{x}^{2})\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =1-2xz \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}-\\frac{2xz}{1+{x}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{2x}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,z & =\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{2x}{1+{x}^{2}},\\,Q(x)=\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,.\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)dx} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int 2x/(1+{x}^{2})dx} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{x}^{2})} \\\\  & =1+{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the equation in standard form by $I(x)=1+{x}^{2}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1+{x}^{2})\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{2x}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,z) & =(1+{x}^{2})\\cdot (\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}) \\\\ (1+{x}^{2})\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+2xz & =1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[z(1+{x}^{2})] & =1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[z(1+{x}^{2})]\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ z(1+{x}^{2}) & =x+C \\\\ z & =\\frac{x+C}{1+{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine $y(x)$ by writing $z={y}^{2}.$ This results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =\\frac{x+C}{1+{x}^{2}} \\\\ y & =\\sqrt{\\frac{x+C}{1+{x}^{2}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere in the last step we used that $y>0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043377/state.json" "file_sha256" "de6f5601e572e1df01884569e7966a5eb04e4de25587a1d15b5e8d41ae8120a0"} {"question" "q-17546" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043377/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "735b4c6e66964dc742ea9ae660ff8a26654509de6cde745dd5f9c25d42d1ddf3"} {"question" "q-17546" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043377/state.json" "file_sha256" "de6f5601e572e1df01884569e7966a5eb04e4de25587a1d15b5e8d41ae8120a0"} {"question" "q-17546" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=\\frac{2x}{C-{x}^{2}}"] ["math" "y=\\frac{x+C}{1+{x}^{2}}"] ["math" "y=\\sqrt{\\frac{2x+C}{2+{x}^{2}}}"] ["math" "y=\\sqrt{\\frac{x+C}{1+x}}"] ["math" "y=\\sqrt{\\frac{x+C}{1+{x}^{2}}}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043377/state.json" "file_sha256" "de6f5601e572e1df01884569e7966a5eb04e4de25587a1d15b5e8d41ae8120a0"} {"question" "q-17546" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=\\sqrt{\\frac{x+C}{1+{x}^{2}}}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043377/state.json" "file_sha256" "de6f5601e572e1df01884569e7966a5eb04e4de25587a1d15b5e8d41ae8120a0"} {"question" "q-17544" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the general solution to the following equation. \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{y+2{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}{x}\n$$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the substitution $z={y}^{-1}.$*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043377/state.json" "file_sha256" "de6f5601e572e1df01884569e7966a5eb04e4de25587a1d15b5e8d41ae8120a0"} {"question" "q-17544" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{-1}\\,⟹\\,y={z}^{-1}\\,\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nConsequently, the original differential equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{z}^{-1}+2{x}^{2}{z}^{-2}}{x} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{xz}+\\frac{2x}{{z}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{z}{x}-2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is linear, so we can use the method of integrating factors. First, we write the above equation in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{z}{x}-2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{x}z & =-2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x)=\\frac{1}{x},\\,Q(x)=-2x\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (1/x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the standard form equation by $I(x)=x$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{x}z) & =x\\cdot (-2x) \\\\ x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+z & =-2{x}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(xz) & =-2{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(xz)\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\int 2{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ xz & =-\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}+{C}_{1} \\\\ z & =\\frac{C-2{x}^{3}}{3x},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=3{C}_{1}.$\n\nFinally, we determine $y(x)$ by writing $z={y}^{-1}.$ This results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{-1} & =\\frac{C-2{x}^{3}}{3x} \\\\ y & =\\frac{3x}{C-2{x}^{3}}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043377/state.json" "file_sha256" "de6f5601e572e1df01884569e7966a5eb04e4de25587a1d15b5e8d41ae8120a0"} {"question" "q-17544" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043377/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "735b4c6e66964dc742ea9ae660ff8a26654509de6cde745dd5f9c25d42d1ddf3"} {"question" "q-17544" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043377/state.json" "file_sha256" "de6f5601e572e1df01884569e7966a5eb04e4de25587a1d15b5e8d41ae8120a0"} {"question" "q-17544" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=\\frac{2x}{C-3{x}^{2}}"] ["math" "y=\\frac{2x}{C-x}"] ["math" "y=\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{C-3x}"] ["math" "y=\\frac{3x}{C-2{x}^{3}}"] ["math" "y=\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{C-{x}^{3}}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043377/state.json" "file_sha256" "de6f5601e572e1df01884569e7966a5eb04e4de25587a1d15b5e8d41ae8120a0"} {"question" "q-17544" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=\\frac{3x}{C-2{x}^{3}}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043377/state.json" "file_sha256" "de6f5601e572e1df01884569e7966a5eb04e4de25587a1d15b5e8d41ae8120a0"} {"question" "q-17552" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the general solution to the following equation.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}={y}^{2}-y\n$$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the substitution* $z={y}^{-1}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043377/state.json" "file_sha256" "de6f5601e572e1df01884569e7966a5eb04e4de25587a1d15b5e8d41ae8120a0"} {"question" "q-17552" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{-1}\\,⟹\\,y={z}^{-1}\\,\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ with the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, the original differential equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{z} \\\\ -\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =1-z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is linear, so we can use the method of integrating factors. First, we write the above equation in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =-1+z \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-z & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x)=-1,\\,Q(x)=-1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (-1)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the standard form equation by $I(x)={e}^{-x}$ yields:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-x}\\cdot (\\frac{dz}{dx}-z) & ={e}^{-x}\\cdot (-1) \\\\ {e}^{-x}\\frac{dz}{dx}-{e}^{-x}z & =-{e}^{-x} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dx}(z{e}^{-x}) & =-{e}^{-x}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dx}(z{e}^{-x})\\,dx\\, & =-\\int {e}^{-x}\\,dx \\\\ z{e}^{-x} & ={e}^{-x}+C \\\\ z & =1+C{e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine $y(x)$ by writing $z={y}^{-1}.$ This results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{-1} & =1+C{e}^{x} \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{1+C{e}^{x}}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043377/state.json" "file_sha256" "de6f5601e572e1df01884569e7966a5eb04e4de25587a1d15b5e8d41ae8120a0"} {"question" "q-17552" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043377/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "735b4c6e66964dc742ea9ae660ff8a26654509de6cde745dd5f9c25d42d1ddf3"} {"question" "q-17552" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043377/state.json" "file_sha256" "de6f5601e572e1df01884569e7966a5eb04e4de25587a1d15b5e8d41ae8120a0"} {"question" "q-17552" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=-\\frac{1}{x+C}"] ["math" "y=\\frac{-1}{1+C{e}^{x}}"] ["math" "y=\\frac{1}{-1+Cx}"] ["math" "y=\\frac{1}{-1+C{e}^{x}}"] ["math" "y=\\frac{1}{1+C{e}^{x}}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043377/state.json" "file_sha256" "de6f5601e572e1df01884569e7966a5eb04e4de25587a1d15b5e8d41ae8120a0"} {"question" "q-17552" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=\\frac{1}{1+C{e}^{x}}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043377/state.json" "file_sha256" "de6f5601e572e1df01884569e7966a5eb04e4de25587a1d15b5e8d41ae8120a0"} {"question" "q-168138" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}\\,\\frac{dy}{dx}=3{y}^{4}+2xy,\\,y(1)=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the fact that the given differential equation can be transformed into the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{6}{x}\\,z=-\\frac{9}{{x}^{2}}\n$$\n\nusing the substitution $z={y}^{-3}.$*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043377/state.json" "file_sha256" "de6f5601e572e1df01884569e7966a5eb04e4de25587a1d15b5e8d41ae8120a0"} {"question" "q-168138" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The substitution $z={y}^{-3}$ transforms the given equation into the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{6}{x}z=-\\frac{9}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nThe above equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{6}{x},\\,Q(x)=-\\frac{9}{{x}^{2}}\\,.\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int 6/x\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{6\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{6}} \\\\  & ={x}^{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nunder the condition that $x>0.$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the equation in standard form by $I(x)={x}^{6}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{6}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{6}{x}\\,z) & ={x}^{6}\\cdot (-\\frac{9}{{x}^{2}}) \\\\ {x}^{6}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+6{x}^{5}z & =-9{x}^{4} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[{x}^{6}z] & =-9{x}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[{x}^{6}z]\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int -9{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {x}^{6}z & =-\\frac{9}{5}{x}^{5}+{C}_{1} \\\\ z & =\\frac{C-9{x}^{5}}{5{x}^{6}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=5{C}_{1}.$\n\nNext, we determine $y(x)$ by writing $z={y}^{-3}.$ This results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{-3} & =\\frac{C-9{x}^{5}}{5{x}^{6}} \\\\ {y}^{3} & =\\frac{5{x}^{6}}{C-9{x}^{5}} \\\\ y & =\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{5{x}^{6}}{C-9{x}^{5}}}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the initial condition  $y(1)=\\frac{1}{2}$ we determine $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & =\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{5}{C-9}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{8} & =\\frac{5}{C-9} \\\\ C-9 & =40 \\\\ C & =49.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{5{x}^{6}}{49-9{x}^{5}}}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043377/state.json" "file_sha256" "de6f5601e572e1df01884569e7966a5eb04e4de25587a1d15b5e8d41ae8120a0"} {"question" "q-168138" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043377/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "735b4c6e66964dc742ea9ae660ff8a26654509de6cde745dd5f9c25d42d1ddf3"} {"question" "q-168138" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043377/state.json" "file_sha256" "de6f5601e572e1df01884569e7966a5eb04e4de25587a1d15b5e8d41ae8120a0"} {"question" "q-168138" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt[3]{9-{x}^{5}}}"] ["math" "y=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt[3]{12-4{x}^{5}}}"] ["math" "y=\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{3x}{25{x}^{3}-1}}"] ["math" "y=\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{5{x}^{6}}{49-9{x}^{5}}}"] ["math" "y=\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{{x}^{6}}{{x}^{5}+7}}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043377/state.json" "file_sha256" "de6f5601e572e1df01884569e7966a5eb04e4de25587a1d15b5e8d41ae8120a0"} {"question" "q-168138" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{5{x}^{6}}{49-9{x}^{5}}}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043377/state.json" "file_sha256" "de6f5601e572e1df01884569e7966a5eb04e4de25587a1d15b5e8d41ae8120a0"} {"question" "q-17556" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{y}{x}+x{y}^{2},\\,y(1)=2,\\,x>0.\n$$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the fact that the given differential equation can be transformed into the equation*\n\n$$\n\\frac{dz}{dx}+\\frac{1}{x}z=-x\n$$\n\n*using the substitution $z={y}^{-1}.$*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043377/state.json" "file_sha256" "de6f5601e572e1df01884569e7966a5eb04e4de25587a1d15b5e8d41ae8120a0"} {"question" "q-17556" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The substitution $z={y}^{-1}$ transforms the given equation into the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dz}{dx}+\\frac{1}{x}z=-x.\n$$\n\nThe above equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x)=\\frac{1}{x},\\,Q(x)=-x\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (1/x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nunder the condition that $x>0.$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the standard form equation by $I(x)=x$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot (\\frac{dz}{dx}+\\frac{1}{x}z) & =x\\cdot (-x) \\\\ x\\cdot \\frac{dz}{dx}+z & =-{x}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dx}(xz) & =-{x}^{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dx}(xz)\\,dx & =-\\int {x}^{2}dx \\\\ xz & =-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+{C}_{1} \\\\ z & =\\frac{C-{x}^{3}}{3x}\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=3{C}_{1}.$\n\nNow, we determine $y(x)$ by writing $z={y}^{-1}.$ This results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{-1} & =\\frac{C-{x}^{3}}{3x} \\\\ y & =\\frac{3x}{C-{x}^{3}}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now determine $C$ from the initial condition $y(1)=2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =\\frac{3}{C-1}\\,⟹\\,C=\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{3x}{(\\frac{5}{2}-{x}^{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{6x}{5-2{x}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043377/state.json" "file_sha256" "de6f5601e572e1df01884569e7966a5eb04e4de25587a1d15b5e8d41ae8120a0"} {"question" "q-17556" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043377/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "735b4c6e66964dc742ea9ae660ff8a26654509de6cde745dd5f9c25d42d1ddf3"} {"question" "q-17556" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043377/state.json" "file_sha256" "de6f5601e572e1df01884569e7966a5eb04e4de25587a1d15b5e8d41ae8120a0"} {"question" "q-17556" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=\\frac{2x}{2-{x}^{3}}"] ["math" "y=\\frac{2x}{3-2{x}^{2}}"] ["math" "y=\\frac{2}{x}"] ["math" "y=\\frac{6x}{5-2{x}^{3}}"] ["math" "y=\\frac{x}{5-2{x}^{3}}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043377/state.json" "file_sha256" "de6f5601e572e1df01884569e7966a5eb04e4de25587a1d15b5e8d41ae8120a0"} {"question" "q-17556" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=\\frac{6x}{5-2{x}^{3}}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043377/state.json" "file_sha256" "de6f5601e572e1df01884569e7966a5eb04e4de25587a1d15b5e8d41ae8120a0"} {"question" "q-17555" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}-\\frac{y(y-1)}{x}=0,\\,y(-1)=2,\\,x<0.\n$$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the fact that the given differential equation can be transformed into the equation*\n\n$$\n\\frac{dz}{dx}-\\frac{1}{x}z=-\\frac{1}{x}\n$$\n\n*using the substitution $z={y}^{-1}.$*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043377/state.json" "file_sha256" "de6f5601e572e1df01884569e7966a5eb04e4de25587a1d15b5e8d41ae8120a0"} {"question" "q-17555" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The substitution $z={y}^{-1}$ transforms the given equation into the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dz}{dx}-\\frac{z}{x}=-\\frac{1}{x}\\,.\n$$\n\nThe above equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x)=-\\frac{1}{x},\\,Q(x)=-\\frac{1}{x}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{-\\int (1/x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x{|}^{-1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{|x|} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nunder the condition that $x<0.$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the standard form equation by $I(x)=-\\frac{1}{x}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{x}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{1}{x}z) & =-\\frac{1}{x}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{x}) \\\\ -\\frac{1}{x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}z & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{x}z) & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{x}z)\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{x}z & =\\frac{1}{x}+C \\\\ z & =1+Cx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine $y(x)$ by writing $z={y}^{-1}.$ This results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{-1} & =1+Cx \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{1+Cx}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now determine $C$ from the initial condition $y(-1)=2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =\\frac{1}{1-C}\\,⟹\\,C=\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{(1+\\frac{1}{2}x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{2+x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043377/state.json" "file_sha256" "de6f5601e572e1df01884569e7966a5eb04e4de25587a1d15b5e8d41ae8120a0"} {"question" "q-17555" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043377/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "735b4c6e66964dc742ea9ae660ff8a26654509de6cde745dd5f9c25d42d1ddf3"} {"question" "q-17555" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043377/state.json" "file_sha256" "de6f5601e572e1df01884569e7966a5eb04e4de25587a1d15b5e8d41ae8120a0"} {"question" "q-17555" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=-\\frac{{e}^{x}}{x+2}"] ["math" "y=\\frac{2}{2+x}"] ["math" "y=\\frac{3-x}{1-x}"] ["math" "y=\\frac{4}{1-x}"] ["math" "y=\\frac{x}{1+{e}^{x}}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043377/state.json" "file_sha256" "de6f5601e572e1df01884569e7966a5eb04e4de25587a1d15b5e8d41ae8120a0"} {"question" "q-17555" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=\\frac{2}{2+x}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043377/state.json" "file_sha256" "de6f5601e572e1df01884569e7966a5eb04e4de25587a1d15b5e8d41ae8120a0"} {"question" "q-17559" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve the initial value problem \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{y(y-1)}{x}=0,\\,y(1)=5,\\,x>0.\n$$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the fact that the given differential equation can be transformed into the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{x}z=\\frac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nusing the substitution $z={y}^{-1}.$*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043377/state.json" "file_sha256" "de6f5601e572e1df01884569e7966a5eb04e4de25587a1d15b5e8d41ae8120a0"} {"question" "q-17559" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The substitution $z={y}^{-1}$ transforms the given equation into the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{x}z=\\frac{1}{x}.\n$$\n\nThe above equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x)=\\frac{1}{x},\\,Q(x)=\\frac{1}{x}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (1/x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nunder the condition that $x>0.$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the standard form equation by $I(x)=x$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{x}z) & =x\\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\ x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+z & =1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(xz) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(xz)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ xz & =x+C \\\\ z & =\\frac{x+C}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine $y(x)$ by writing $z={y}^{-1}.$ This results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{-1} & =\\frac{x+C}{x} \\\\ y & =\\frac{x}{x+C}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now determine $C$ from the initial condition  $y(1)=5:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5 & =\\frac{1}{1+C}\\,⟹\\,C=-\\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{x}{(x-\\frac{4}{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{5x}{(5x-4)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043377/state.json" "file_sha256" "de6f5601e572e1df01884569e7966a5eb04e4de25587a1d15b5e8d41ae8120a0"} {"question" "q-17559" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043377/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "735b4c6e66964dc742ea9ae660ff8a26654509de6cde745dd5f9c25d42d1ddf3"} {"question" "q-17559" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043377/state.json" "file_sha256" "de6f5601e572e1df01884569e7966a5eb04e4de25587a1d15b5e8d41ae8120a0"} {"question" "q-17559" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=\\frac{3x+7}{1+x}"] ["math" "y=\\frac{5x}{5x-4}"] ["math" "y=\\frac{x+4}{4x-3}"] ["math" "y=\\frac{x-6}{x-2}"] ["math" "y=\\frac{x}{x+1}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043377/state.json" "file_sha256" "de6f5601e572e1df01884569e7966a5eb04e4de25587a1d15b5e8d41ae8120a0"} {"question" "q-17559" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=\\frac{5x}{5x-4}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043377/state.json" "file_sha256" "de6f5601e572e1df01884569e7966a5eb04e4de25587a1d15b5e8d41ae8120a0"} {"question" "q-79845" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\nx\\frac{dy}{dx}+(x+1)y=\\frac{3}{xy},\\,y(1)=\\sqrt{3},\\,x>0,\\,y>0.\n$$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the fact that the given differential equation can be transformed into the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+2z=6\n$$\n\nusing the substitution $z={x}^{2}{y}^{2}.$*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043377/state.json" "file_sha256" "de6f5601e572e1df01884569e7966a5eb04e4de25587a1d15b5e8d41ae8120a0"} {"question" "q-79845" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The substitution $z={x}^{2}{y}^{2}$ transforms the given equation into the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+2z=6.\n$$\n\nThe above equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\nP(x)=2,\\,Q(x)=6\\,.\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int 2\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the equation in standard form by $I(x)={e}^{2x}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2x}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+2z) & ={e}^{2x}\\cdot 6 \\\\ {e}^{2x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+2{e}^{2x}z & =6{e}^{2x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{2x}z) & =6{e}^{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{2x}z)\\,\\text{d}x & =6\\int {e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {e}^{2x}z & =3{e}^{2x}+C \\\\ z & =3+C{e}^{-2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine $y(x)$ by writing $z={x}^{2}{y}^{2}.$ This results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{y}^{2} & =3+C{e}^{-2x} \\\\ {y}^{2} & =\\frac{3+C{e}^{-2x}}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ y & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3+C{e}^{-2x}}}{x}\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we used that $x>0$ and $y>0.$\n\nFrom the initial condition $y(1)=\\sqrt{3}$ we determine $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3+C{e}^{-2}}}{1}\\,⟹\\,C=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{x}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043377/state.json" "file_sha256" "de6f5601e572e1df01884569e7966a5eb04e4de25587a1d15b5e8d41ae8120a0"} {"question" "q-79845" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043377/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "735b4c6e66964dc742ea9ae660ff8a26654509de6cde745dd5f9c25d42d1ddf3"} {"question" "q-79845" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043377/state.json" "file_sha256" "de6f5601e572e1df01884569e7966a5eb04e4de25587a1d15b5e8d41ae8120a0"} {"question" "q-79845" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=\\frac{1+3{e}^{x}}{x}"] ["math" "y=\\frac{3+{e}^{-2x}}{x}"] ["math" "y=\\frac{4-x}{\\sqrt{3}x}"] ["math" "y=\\frac{5-2x}{\\sqrt{3}x}"] ["math" "y=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{x}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043377/state.json" "file_sha256" "de6f5601e572e1df01884569e7966a5eb04e4de25587a1d15b5e8d41ae8120a0"} {"question" "q-79845" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{x}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043377/state.json" "file_sha256" "de6f5601e572e1df01884569e7966a5eb04e4de25587a1d15b5e8d41ae8120a0"} {"question" "e-6867" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The substitution $z= y^2$ can be used to transform the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{{\\rm{d}}y}}{{{\\rm{d}}x}} = \\dfrac{x+2}{2y} + 2x^2y\n$$\n\ninto an equation of the form $z'(x) + P(x)z(x) = Q(x).$ What is the function $P(x)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043377/example-6867.json" "file_sha256" "d9b9cf96b7080db6211f550b33f6310bfb4e277164c042ed351e2ad47a31ea5c"} {"question" "e-6867" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz= y^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}  = 2y \\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{1}{2z^{1/2}}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nConsequently, the original differential equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2{z}^{1/2}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{x+2}{2{z}^{1/2}}+2{x}^{2}{z}^{1/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now write the above equation in standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} + P(x)z = Q(x).\n$$\n\nIn doing so, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =x+2+4{x}^{2}z \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-4{x}^{2}z & =x+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x) = - 4x^2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043377/example-6867.json" "file_sha256" "d9b9cf96b7080db6211f550b33f6310bfb4e277164c042ed351e2ad47a31ea5c"} {"question" "e-6606" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the general solution to the following first-order ODE using the substitution $z=y^3.$\n\n$$\nxy^2\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x} = x^2+y^3\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043377/example-6606.json" "file_sha256" "bffffbd70468ba92aae9aa75f368c0aee402257f4c8263245d0641762faddf2a"} {"question" "e-6606" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's make a substitution\n\n$$\nz=y^3\\,.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}=3y^2\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\ny^2\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}=\\dfrac{1}{3}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\n Consequently, the original differential equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{3}\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}\\right)=x^2+z\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is linear, so we can use the method of integrating factors. First, we write the above equation in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{3}{x}({x}^{2}+z) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =3x+\\frac{3}{x}z \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{3}{x}\\,z & =3x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} + P(x)y = Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x)=-\\frac{3}{x},\\,Q(x)=3x\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (-3/x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the standard form equation by $I(x)=\\dfrac{1}{x^3}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{3}{x}\\,z) & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\cdot 3x \\\\ \\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{3}{{x}^{4}}\\,z & =\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{z}{{x}^{3}}) & =\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{z}{{x}^{3}})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{z}{{x}^{3}} & =-\\frac{3}{x}+C \\\\ z & =-3{x}^{2}+C{x}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine $y(x)$ by substituting the solution into $z=y^3.$ This results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{3} & =z \\\\ {y}^{3} & =-3{x}^{2}+C{x}^{3} \\\\ y & =\\sqrt[3]{-3{x}^{2}+C{x}^{3}}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043377/example-6606.json" "file_sha256" "bffffbd70468ba92aae9aa75f368c0aee402257f4c8263245d0641762faddf2a"} {"question" "e-6605" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the specific solution of the first-order ODE\n\n$$\nxe^y\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x}+e^y = e^{x} \\, ,\n$$\n\ngiven that $y(1)=1$ and $x>0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043377/example-6605.json" "file_sha256" "6e85c23c994e28a56c577ed5228794d46f06d41d2599fffd232874de2a7cc6a8"} {"question" "e-6605" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's make a substitution\n\n$$\nz=e^y\\,.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ with the chain rule, we get $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}=e^y\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}.$ Consequently, the original differential equation can be written in terms of  $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}+z=e^x\\,,\n$$\n\nwith the initial condition\n\n$$\nz(1)=e^{y(1)}=e^1=e\\,.\n$$\n\nNotice that the left-hand side of the differential equation is now an exact derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+z & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(xz)\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(xz) & ={e}^{x} \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(xz)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{x}\\text{d}x \\\\ xz & ={e}^{x}+C \\\\ z & =\\frac{{e}^{x}+C}{x}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we determine the value of $C$ that satisfies the initial condition $z(1)=e\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z(1) & =e \\\\ \\frac{{e}^{1}+C}{1} & =e \\\\ e+C & =e \\\\ C & =0\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have \n\n$$\nz  =   \\frac{e^x}{x}\\,.\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine $y(x)$ by substituting the solution into $z=e^y.$ This results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{y} & =z \\\\ {e}^{y} & =\\frac{{e}^{x}}{x} \\\\ y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{e}^{x}}{x}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{x})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x) \\\\  & =x-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nunder the condition that $x>0.$ So, the final solution is\n\n$$\ny = x - \\ln (x), \\quad x > 0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043377/example-6605.json" "file_sha256" "6e85c23c994e28a56c577ed5228794d46f06d41d2599fffd232874de2a7cc6a8"}] "usage" {"e-6867" [] "q-17544" [] "e-6606" [] "e-6605" [] "q-17559" [] "q-17546" []} "basis" 1613 "retractions" [[17592186101165 :question/problem "Find the general solution to the following equation.\n$2(1 + x^{2})\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{1}{y} - 2xy, y > 0$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the substitution $z = y^{2}$.*"] [17592186101163 :question/problem "Find the general solution to the following equation.\n$\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{y + 2x^{2}y^{2}}{x}$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the substitution $z = y^{-1}$.*"] [17592186101168 :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n$\\frac{dy}{dx} + (y(y - 1))/(x) = 0, y(1) = 5, x > 0$.\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the fact that the given differential equation can be transformed into the equation\n$\\frac{dz}{dx} + \\frac{1}{x}z = \\frac{1}{x}$\nusing the substitution $z = y^{-1}$.*"] [17592186100676 :question/problem "The substitution $z= y^2$ can be used to transform the equation\n$\\dfrac{{{\\rm{d}}y}}{{{\\rm{d}}x}} = \\dfrac{x+2}{2y} + 2x^2y$\ninto an equation of the form $z'(x) + P(x)z(x) = Q(x)$. What is the function $P(x)$?"] [17592186100676 :question/worked-solution "First, we make the substitution\n$z= y^{2}$.\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} = 2y \\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{1}{2z^{1/2}}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the original differential equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{2z^{1/2}}\\frac{dz}{dx} &= \\frac{x + 2}{2z^{1/2}} + 2x^{2}z^{1/2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now write the above equation in standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} + P(x)z = Q(x)\n$$\n\nIn doing so, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dz}{dx} &= x + 2 + 4x^{2}z \\\\\n\\frac{dz}{dx} - 4x^{2}z &= x + 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(x) =-4x^2\n$$"] [17592186100671 :question/problem "Find the general solution to the following first-order ODE using the substitution $z=y^3$.\n$xy^2\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x} = x^2+y^3$"] [17592186100671 :question/worked-solution "Let's make a substitution\n$z=y^3\\,$.\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}=3y^2\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad y^2\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}=\\dfrac{1}{3}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the original differential equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{3}\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}\\right)=x^2+z\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is linear, so we can use the method of integrating factors. First, we write the above equation in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dz}{dx} &= \\frac{3}{x}(x^{2} + z) \\\\\n\\frac{dz}{dx} &= 3x + \\frac{3}{x}z \\\\\n\\frac{dz}{dx} - \\frac{3}{x}z &= 3x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} + P(x)y = Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\nP(x) =-\\frac{3}{x}, Q(x) = 3x\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI(x) &= e^{∫P(x)dx} \\\\\n&= e^{∫(-3/x)dx} \\\\\n&= e^{-3\\ln x} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{x^{3}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the standard form equation by\n\n$$\nI(x)=\\dfrac{1}{x^3}\n$$\n\nyields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{x^{3}}(\\frac{dz}{dx} - \\frac{3}{x}z) &= \\frac{1}{x^{3}} \\cdot 3x \\\\\n\\frac{1}{x^{3}} \\cdot \\frac{dz}{dx} - \\frac{3}{x^{4}}z &= \\frac{3}{x^{2}} \\\\\n\\frac{d}{dx}(\\frac{z}{x^{3}}) &= \\frac{3}{x^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫\\frac{d}{dx}(\\frac{z}{x^{3}})dx &= ∫\\frac{3}{x^{2}}dx \\\\\n\\frac{z}{x^{3}} &= -\\frac{3}{x} + C \\\\\nz &= -3x^{2} + Cx^{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine $y(x)$ by substituting the solution into $z=y^3$. This results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{3} &= z \\\\\ny^{3} &= -3x^{2} + Cx^{3} \\\\\ny &= \\sqrt[3]{-3x^{2} + Cx^{3}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186100670 :question/problem "Find the specific solution of the first-order ODE\n$xe^y\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x}+e^y = e^{x} \\,$,\ngiven that $y(1)=1$ and $x>0$."] [17592186100670 :question/worked-solution "Let's make a substitution\n$z=e^y\\,$.\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ with the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}=e^y\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the original differential equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}+z=e^x\\,\n$$\n\nwith the initial condition\n\n$$\nz(1)=e^{y(1)}=e^1=e\\,\n$$\n\nNotice that the left-hand side of the differential equation is now an exact derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx\\frac{dz}{dx} + z &= \\frac{d}{dx}(xz)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dx}(xz) &= e^{x} \\\\\n∫\\frac{d}{dx}(xz)dx &= ∫e^{x}dx \\\\\nxz &= e^{x} + C \\\\\nz &= \\frac{e^{x} + C}{x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we determine the value of $C$ that satisfies the initial condition $z(1)=e$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nz(1) &= e \\\\\n\\frac{e^{1} + C}{1} &= e \\\\\ne + C &= e \\\\\nC &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have\n\n$$\nz = \\frac{e^x}{x}\\,\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine $y(x)$ by substituting the solution into $z=e^y$. This results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ne^{y} &= z \\\\\ne^{y} &= \\frac{e^{x}}{x} \\\\\ny &= \\ln (\\frac{e^{x}}{x}) \\\\\n&= \\ln (e^{x}) - \\ln (x) \\\\\n&= x - \\ln (x)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nunder the condition that $x>0$. So, the final solution is\n\n$$\ny = x - \\ln (x), \\quad x > 0\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186098745 17592186098746 17592186098747 17592186098748 17592186098749 17592186098757 17592186098758 17592186098759 17592186098760 17592186098761 17592186098775 17592186098776 17592186098777 17592186098778 17592186098779] "immutable_fields" [[17592186098744 :answer-field/correct] [17592186098744 :answer-field/id] [17592186098744 :answer-field/key] [17592186098744 :answer-field/type] [17592186098756 :answer-field/correct] [17592186098756 :answer-field/id] [17592186098756 :answer-field/key] [17592186098756 :answer-field/type] [17592186098774 :answer-field/correct] [17592186098774 :answer-field/id] [17592186098774 :answer-field/key] [17592186098774 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
